### 1. Rationale

The intervention replaces temporal self-attention (O(L^2), pairwise token mixing, global but data-dependent, position via absolute/rotary) with a non-causal Hyena operator:
- Hyena = interleaved input-dependent gating + data-independent long convolution h generated by a small CNN from positions; FFT-based circular convolution -> O(L log L).
- The long convolution is exactly the discrete-time representation of an SSM (family with S4/H3): a filter h spanning the whole sequence length, so each output token mixes information over the full context in O(L log L) instead of O(L^2).
- Non-causal (two-sided, symmetric/bidirectional) filters: unlike causal Hyena (filter truncated to present for streaming), non-causal sees the whole block, which suits masked blockwise generation of trajectories and gives symmetric local-neighborhood receptive fields.
- Multiscale heads: multiple filters of different effective scales/lengths => a filter bank, giving the operator an intrinsic multi-timescale inductive bias (relevant to "capture dynamics over multiple timescales").
- Hypothese: (H1) length scalability — attention's O(L^2) cost and degraded long-range mixing cause earlier "consistency breakpoint"; Hyena's O(L log L) + full-context long convolution delays it. (H2) multi-timescale fidelity — Hyena's multiscale filter bank preserves per-band spectral fidelity to longer horizons; but risk: fixed (data-independent) filters + smooth CNN-generated spectra may cause spectral rolloff / loss of fine local detail, which attention might handle better at short horizons. (H3) non-causality is necessary for the benefit — causal Hyena should degrade (no look-ahead) vs non-causal, isolating the causality factor.

### 2. Architecture

Common backbone (both arms), SiT-style:
- Input: trajectory state sequence x_{1..L} with d_state dims, optionally patchified (patch = temporal block/stride) into tokens with position encoding.
- SiT conditioning: continuous time/timestep t embedded + adaLN-Zero injected into each block.
- Block: pre-norm -> temporal mixing module -> MLP (two-layer, GELU), with residual.
- Final head: decode velocity field / denoising target; or next state.

setup_4 (baseline, "original time attention"): temporal mixing = multi-head self-attention; explicit design decision: use non-causal attention over the whole context block, with rotary position encoding (locked as design decision since causality of baseline is unknown — make both arms comparable; note the alternative causal baseline as a control). Actually since both arms generate with blockwise fill/extend, non-causal attention is natural. But we also add a causal-attention control.

Wait — need to be careful. Block-based generation with non-causal attention: during training, attention can attend to all positions within the block (masked only to not attend to "future" target? In masked generation, the model sees a block containing context + some generated portion and predicts continuation). Hmm. Let me define the generation protocol clearly.

Generation protocol (identical for both arms): blockwise "fill-and-extend". The model is trained on a masked objective: given an observed prefix of length L_context within a length-L window, predict the remaining suffix (the interpolation/continuation). During generation: 
- Seed with the true condition C (context).
- Generate an initial block of future tokens length B by running the model on the window [C_1..C_Lc, MASK...] and predicting the masked region (fill).
- Then slide/"extend": take the last K generated tokens (plus a trailing context) as new condition, run the model again to predict the next block, re-injecting generated tokens. Continue until G total steps.
- Both arms use this same protocol. Non-causal operators (Hyena non-causal, non-causal attention) attend within the current window block; at each block they have full access to the block (context + masked positions to fill) — i.e., they are non-causal within block but causal across blocks (blockwise autoregressive).

Actually to keep "non-causal" meaningful: within a block, non-causal operators see both sides. Because future within the block is being predicted, we need the block to contain context (observed) and the masked continuation; the model fills the continuation using bidirectional context within the block. For the next block, generated tokens become part of the observed block. This is like "mask-conditioned infilling per block, iterated". This works identically for attention and Hyena arms.

For a causal control: causal attention/Hyena predicts strictly autoregressively one position at a time with only past tokens.

Training objective: SiT interpolant — sample random t ∈ [0,1], x_t = α(t)·x_data + σ(t)·x_noise over the target region, regress velocity v = d x_t/dt (or a parametrization), conditioned on the observed region. Equivalent to masked denoising. Alternatively simpler MSE next-token. But SiT implies interpolant/flow. I'll specify SiT flow training with masked (observed-region conditioned) denoising over the future region. Also mention simpler "teacher-forced next-token MSE" as a compatibility check (both arms same).

Controls:
- C1: causal Hyena (same backbone; causal filter — isolate causality).
- C2: subquadratic/local-window attention: e.g., sliding window attention with window W << L (isolate "subquadratic" vs "operator type"); or linear attention (Performers). 
- C3: causal attention baseline (isolates causality for the baseline).
- Optionally a matched-parameter ablation: reduce attention head dims to match Hyena param count exactly; and a training-steps-matched abatement (all arms run same # steps; also record a "same wall-clock" variant).
- C4 (orthogonal): multiscale-head count sensitivity: vary # Hyena heads/filter lengths (sweep) — this is more an analysis than a control.

Parameter/training budget matching: all arms same backbone dims, same block count; the temporal module differences naturally change params slightly — match by adjusting MLP hidden width or head dims; fix total trainable params to within ±2%; same optimizer (AdamW), same LR schedule (e.g., cosine w/ warmup), same batch size, same #train steps, same seeds, same data.

### 3. Data

Synthetic simulator (primary):
- S1 MultiSine: y(t) = Σ_{i=1..3} A_i sin(2π f_i t + φ_i) + slow linear drift + Gaussian noise; frequencies well separated (e.g., f = 0.5, 0.05, 0.005 cycles/step); sample at dt with multi-timescale structure; known PSD.
- S2 Coupled harmonic oscillators: 2DOF spring-mass with two natural frequencies & coupling; analytic solution; energy invariant.
- S3 Lorenz-96: chaotic, 40-dim state; long trajectories stable within attractor; "consistency" = staying on attractor (boundedness), not collapsing/diverging.
- S4 Mackey-Glass: chaotic delay ODE; known single-scale-ish; alternative.
- S5 Damped pendulum with slow forcing envelope: fast oscillation + slow amplitude modulation (two timescales).
- All: generate training data with multiple system instances/parameter draws; split by instance (no time-leakage); normalize per-instance; fixed seeds per fold.

Realistic (secondary, if obtainable): AMASS human motion capture (joint-angle trajectories) and a weather reanalysis channel (e.g., single-location ERA5 time series) — long, multi-timescale, real noise. Optional robustness check; if not available, design proceeds on synthetic only (state explicitly).

Data regime: choose L_train per task (e.g., 1024 steps synthetic; 512 steps mocap). Sequence truncation; long generation test requires generating G up to 2^14 steps for the "≥2^14" operationalization, hence simulator support (synthetic unbounded). For mocap, long generation test length bounded by data—so primary evidence from synthetic.

### 4. Training/generation protocol (precise)

Training:
- Window-based: sample random windows of length L_train from train split.
- Observed/masked split within window: random contiguous observed region length L_c (e.g., 25-50% of window) + future region masked.
- SiT objective: sample t ~ p(t) (uniform or logit-normal), build interpolant x_t over future region, condition on observed region; minimize ||v_θ(·) − v||² (or equivalent epsilon/target parametrization); condition also on t embedding.
- Batch = 32-64 windows; AdamW; cosine LR w/ 1k warmup; weight decay 0.05; gradient clipping; mixed precision if GPU.
- #steps: set by compute budget; e.g., full-scale 200k steps; pilot 20k.
- Early stopping via validation (held-out instances) on masked-region denoising loss.

Generation (identical protocol across arms):
- Seed: true condition C (length L_c) per test instance.
- Repetition for k=1..K_blocks, each block predicts B tokens:
  - Build current window = [latest observed/context tokens (length B' maybe overlapping) + masked region of length B].
  - Run model once over the window; read predicted future region (fill).
  - Append predicted tokens to generated trajectory; the next block's context = last K_context generated (or mix of true context + generated).
  - Continue until G steps reached.
- Deterministic sampling: first use the mean ODE solution (most-probable trajectory) for headline metric; additionally stochastic SDE sampling for distributional metrics (PSD/entropy) with e.g., 10 samples/instance.
- Record also the "one-shot long window" generation: single forward over a window of length G (when feasible for attention at moderate G) vs the blockwise protocol, to separate protocol artifacts from model capability. This is a nice control: at small G both arms can generate one-shot; compare blockwise vs one-shot.

Note: this protocol is identical for attention and Hyena arms. For non-causal operator arms, within-block bidirectional context; for causal control arms, auto-regressive one-token loop. Block size B and context L_c fixed.

### 5. Metrics & statistics ledger

Pointwise accuracy:
- M1: 1-step MSE / RMSE (normalized by per-instance std) on held-out true continuations (test-set oracle).
- M2: rollout RMSE vs ground truth at G=... (but for chaotic systems MSE saturates — combine with distributional metrics).

Long-range consistency (thresholds in parentheses):
- V1: Divergence time τ_ε = earliest step where |̂y − y| > ε·σ_data (ε=1.0 default; report ε=0.5,1,2 band). Report median and distribution across instances.
- V2: Divergence growth exponent λ (fit log error vs time over a plateau before saturation); expected to separate regimes: λ≈0 (stable/no growth) vs λ>0. For chaotic systems, a well-calibrated model should show growth bounded by the system's own Lyapunov exponent; define "consistent" as λ ≤ λ_system + margin (margin 10%).
- V3: Statistical stationarity: compute rolling window statistics (mean, var, autocorr@lag) over the generated trajectory; report max drift of rolling mean/var from the first-segment value, threshold ≤ 10% of first-segment value.
- V4: Invariant/boundedness preservation: fraction of time steps within physical range (e.g., Lorenz attractor bound, energy within ±5% of initial, no NaN/blow-up); threshold ≥ 0.99 within range; energy drift ≤5%.
- V5: Non-degeneracy: variance ratio Var(generated)/Var(data) ∈ [0.8, 1.2]; mode-collapse index (fraction of mass at mean/spectral whitening); threshold: not collapsed — fraction of near-constant steps ≤ 1%.

