-\n\n## 1. Rationale: why this intervention should matter (mechanistic link)\n\nThe intervention replaces the temporal self-attention in the SiT backbone with a non-causal Hyena operator. The mechanistic differences that motivate the hypothesis:\n\n- **Cost scaling.** Self-attention mixes tokens with O(L\u00b2) time and memory; Hyena mixes with an implicit long convolution computed via FFT in **O(L log L)**. For \"extremely long\" trajectories this is the difference between practically reachable and unreachable windows.\n- **Mixing reach.** Attention is pairwise and data-dependent but its effective receptive field can degrade along very long sequences (attention-entropy concentration, context dilution). Hyena's long convolution is the discrete-time representation of a state-space model (same family as S4/H3): every output position convolves the whole sequence with a filter h, so information reaches the full context by construction, not by learned global attention.\n- **Non-causality.** The causal Hyena variant truncates the filter (streaming/left-context-only). The **non-causal (bidirectional, two-sided) variant** uses symmetric filters and sees the full block; for trajectory generation this gives symmetric local receptive fields and enables mask-conditioned block filling \u2014 while still being O(L log L). The causality factor is separately controlled (Control C1/C3) to attribute any benefit to the operator vs. to non-causality itself.\n- **Multiscale inductive bias.** Hyena uses **multiple heads whose implicit filters have different effective scales (a filter bank)**. This is the direct mechanism by which it could \"capture dynamics over multiple timescales\": separate heads can adapt to fast, mid, and slow modes of the data, checked in Analysis A3 by comparing learned filter spectra to the data PSD.\n\nHypotheses (each falsifiable, tied to metrics in \u00a75):\n- H1 Length scalability: setup_5 reaches a \"consistency breakpoint\" at a larger generation length G than setup_4 (metrics V1\u2013V5).\n- H2 Multi-timescale fidelity: setup_5 preserves per-band spectral/entropy fidelity to longer horizons (F1\u2013F6); setup_4 may match it at short horizons but degrades first.\n- H3 Causality channel: non-causal Hyena outperforms causal Hyena (C1) at long G, isolating causality; and non-causal attention (C3) tests the baseline side.\n- H4 Failure asymmetry: the hypothesized risk of Hyena is data-independent filter smoothness \u2192 spectral roll-off / loss of fine local detail; attention's risk is context degradation \u2192 divergence/collapse at long G (A3).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture specification: the two arms and every control\n\n### 2.1 Shared SiT-style backbone (identical in both arms)\n- Input: trajectory state sequence x_1..L \u2208 R^{L\u00d7d_state}; temporal **patch embedding** (patch = small block of \u03c4 steps, stride \u03c4) \u2192 tokens L_tok = \u230aL/\u03c4\u230b with learned position encoding; linear embedding to model dim d_model.\n- Block (N blocks): pre-norm \u2192 **temporal mixing module** (\u00a72.2/\u00a72.3) \u2192 MLP (2-layer, GELU, hidden 4\u00d7d_model) \u2192 residual. Continuous time/flow variable t embedded (rotary/sinusoidal) and injected via **adaLN-Zero** per block (SiT-style conditioning). This conditioning path is UNTOUCHED by the intervention (design decision, see \u00a79).\n- Output head: predicts the interpolant velocity field v_\u03b8(x_t, t | observed region) (and optionally the equivalent denoising target for the compatibility check).\n\n### 2.2 setup_4 (baseline: original time attention = temporal self-attention)\n- Temporal mixing module = multi-head self-attention (h heads, d_head), rotary position embeddings.\n- **Design decision (locked):** within-block **non-causal** self-attention over the current window block (both directions allowed inside the block), because generation uses the blockwise fill/extend protocol (\u00a74) and this matches the non-causal property of setup_5, keeping the causality factor orthogonal. A causal-attention variant is included as Control C3.\n\n### 2.3 setup_5 (intervention: non-causal Hyena operator)\n- Temporal mixing module = non-causal Hyena: input-dependent gating, data-independent long-convolution filters h_j (j = 1..m) generated by a small position-encoding CNN (so filters are length \u221d context, smooth, parameterized compactly); multi-scale: m heads with filters/effective lengths spanning different scales (e.g., 2^r for r=1..m). Convolution computed via FFT (circular) in O(L log L); **non-causal variant = symmetric/two-sided filters** (no temporal truncation). Drop-in replacement for the attention block with matching residual and norm structure.\n\n### 2.4 Deconfounding controls\n- **C1 Causal Hyena:** identical to setup_5 but with causal filter truncation (streaming, left-context only). Isolates the causality factor on the intervention side.\n- **C2 Subquadratic/local-window attention:** sliding-window attention (window W << L) at matched O(L\u00b7W) cost, plus optional linear (Performer-style) attention. Isolates \"subquadratic operator\" from \"Hyena specifically.\"\n- **C3 Causal attention baseline:** setup_4 with causal masking. Isolates the causality factor on the baseline side.\n- **C4 Parameter/training matching:** all arms matched to within \u00b12% total trainable parameters (adjust MLP width / head dims), same optimizer, LR schedule, steps, batch, seeds.\n- **C5 One-shot vs blockwise:** at small G, a single forward over a window of length G (both arms) used as a protocol sanity control (A5).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Data / simulation environments (self-contained, known ground truth)\n\n### 3.1 Primary: synthetic simulators (unbounded length \u21d2 can test G \u2265 2^14)\n- **S1 Multisine + drift:** y(t) = \u03a3_{i=1..3} A_i sin(2\u03c0 f_i t + \u03c6_i) + slow linear drift + Gaussian noise; f well separated (fast/mid/slow, e.g., 0.5 / 0.05 / 0.005 cycles/step). Known analytic spectrum; direct test of multi-timescale coverage.\n- **S2 Coupled harmonic oscillators:** 2-DOF spring\u2013mass with two natural frequencies and coupling; analytic solution; total-energy invariance \u21d2 invariant-preservation metric.\n- **S3 Lorenz-96 (N=40):** chaotic; long trajectories remain on bounded attractor; \"consistency\" = staying on attractor, no blow-up/collapse; known characteristic Lyapunov time for the divergence-growth bound.\n- **S4 Mackey\u2013Glass (chaotic delay ODE):** second chaotic benchmark; single dominant scale + broadband.\n- **S5 Damped pendulum with slow forcing envelope:** fast oscillation + slow amplitude modulation = clean two-timescale structure.\n- Splits: multiple random system-instance/parameter draws; split by instance (no temporal leakage); per-instance normalization; fixed per-fold seeds.\n\n### 3.2 Secondary: realistic (optional robustness check, only if obtainable)\n- AMASS/SMPL human-motion joint-angle sequences and a single-location weather reanalysis series (long, multi-timescale, real noise). If not obtainable, the design proceeds on synthetic alone (explicitly stated decision).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Training and generation protocol (identical for both arms)\n\n### 4.1 Training\n- **Task:** conditioned trajectory generation. Sample a window of length L_train (synthetic 1024; mocap 512). Split into observed prefix (length L_c \u2248 25\u201350% of window) and masked future region.\n- **SiT interpolant objective:** sample t ~ p(t) (uniform or logit-normal); build interpolant over the future region x_t = \u03b1(t)x_data + \u03c3(t)x_noise with \u03b1,\u03c3 chosen (e.g., as in Tong et al.); condition the model on the observed region + t; regress the velocity field. Equivalent denoising-target parametrization is an allowed variant (compatibility check arm C6 keeps objective fixed between setup_4/setup_5).\n- **Optimization:** AdamW, cosine LR with 1k warmup, weight decay 0.05, gradient clipping, batch 32\u201364 windows; mixed precision if GPU. Step budget: pilot 20k; full 200k. Early stopping on held-out-instance masked-region loss.\n\n### 4.2 Generation \u2014 blockwise FILL/EXTEND rollout (same protocol for BOTH arms)\nNon-causal operators cannot stream one token at a time; both arms therefore generate with an identical blockwise protocol:\n1. Seed: true condition C of length L_c.\n2. For block k = 1..K (each predicts B tokens): assemble the current window = [most recent context (recent true or generated tokens, length B) \u2225 masked region of length B]; run the model once over the window (bidirectional context inside the block); read out the predicted future block (**fill**).\n3. Append generated tokens to the trajectory; next block's context = last B tokens just generated (**extend**); repeat until G total steps.\n4. Two sampling modes: (a) deterministic ODE (mean/most-probable path) for headline pointwise metrics; (b) stochastic SDE, 10 samples/instance, for distributional metrics (PSD/entropy).\n5. Parameters fixed across arms: B, L_c, block-overlap, number of ODE steps per block.\n6. (Control C5) one-shot variant: single forward over a window of length G where feasible, to measure the protocol artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Metrics and statistics ledger (explicit thresholds)\n\n### 5.1 Pointwise accuracy\n- **M1** 1-step RMSE and rolling-rollout RMSE vs. held-out true continuation, normalized by per-instance \u03c3. (Descriptive; not used for the headline claim.)\n\n### 5.2 Long-range consistency (headline for \"consistent\")\n- **V1** Divergence time \u03c4_\u03b5 = earliest step where |\u0177 \u2212 y| > \u03b5\u00b7\u03c3_data; report \u03b5 \u2208 {0.5, 1.0, 2.0}; headline uses \u03b5 = 1.0. Report median + IQR over instances/seeds.\n- **V2** Divergence-growth exponent \u03bb (linear fit of log error over a plateau). Consistency threshold: \u03bb \u2264 \u03bb_system + 10% margin (\u03bb_system = known Lyapunov exponent for chaotic S3/S4; \u03bb=0 for stable S1/S2/S5).\n- **V3** Statistical stationarity: rolling-window mean/var/autocorr-lag-1 over generated trajectory; PASS if max drift from the initial segment \u2264 10% of that segment's value.\n- **V4** Invariant/boundedness preservation: fraction of steps within physical range \u2265 0.99; energy drift \u2264 \u00b15% (S2); no NaN/blow-up.\n- **V5** Non-degeneracy (no collapse to mean / fixed point): Var(gen)/Var(data) \u2208 [0.8, 1.2]; fraction of near-constant steps \u2264 1%.\n\n### 5.3 Multi-timescale coverage (headline for \"capture dynamics over multiple timescales\")\n- **F1** Per-band log-spectral error: 3 bands [fast, mid, slow] (octave bands derived from data spectrum); mean |log10(PSD_gen/PSD_ref)| per band \u2264 0.15 per band at the tested horizon (\u2248 factor 1.4).\n- **F2** Wavelet/scalogram Pearson correlation \u2265 0.8 at matched horizons.\n- **F3** Multiscale entropy (coarse-graining scales 1..10): per-scale |SampEn_gen \u2212 SampEn_ref| \u2264 0.1.\n- **F4** Autocorrelation-decay match: RMSE(log|ACF_gen| vs log|ACF_ref|) over lags \u2264 0.25\u00b7G \u2264 0.1; Hurst exponent |\u0125_gen \u2212 \u0125_ref| \u2264 0.1.\n- **F5** Dominant-mode recovery per band: dominant-frequency relative error \u2264 5% of band width.\n- **F6** Band-limited rollout RMSE: band-passed [fast/mid/slow] RMSE vs. band-passed reference \u2014 identifies which timescale degrades first.\n\n### 5.4 Efficiency (mechanism why Hyena may enable longer G)\n- **E1** Wall-clock and peak memory per generated step at G \u2208 {L_train, 8\u00d7, 64\u00d7, 128\u00d7 L_train} for each arm.\n- **E2** Theoretical complexity note: O(L\u00b2) vs O(L log L).\n\n### 5.5 Statistical protocol\n- \u2265 **5 seeds per config** (target 8 if budget allows); each seed = init + fresh data draw; same seeds across arms.\n- **Paired comparisons**: same-seed pairing setup_4 vs setup_5 and vs each control; report mean \u00b1 95% CI (bootstrap over seeds), plus effect size (Cohen's d) for V1-\u03c4 and F1.\n- **Pre-registered decision rule** (to answer the question as yes/no): setup_5 SUPPORTS the claim iff, at G = 64\u00d7 L_train: (i) median \u03c4_{\u03b5=1} \u2265 2\u00d7 setup_4 AND CI excludes equality; (ii) F1 \u2264 0.15/band AND not worse than setup_4 (within CI) at short horizons; (iii) V3, V4, V5 thresholds all met. Otherwise REJECT or qualify (e.g., \"supports only up to length X\").\n- Multiple-comparison control: Holm correction applied only to the three headline claims; all other metrics reported descriptively.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis plan\n- **A1 Length-scaling:** headline metrics vs G/L_train on log axis; estimate each arm's \"consistency breakpoint\" G* (first violation of V3/V4/V5); compare G* distributions.\n- **A2 Ablation decomposition:** matched-difference summaries \u2014 setup_5 vs C1 (causality), setup_4 vs C3 (baseline causality), setup_4 vs C2 (subquadratic vs Hyena), setup_4 vs setup_5 (operator).\n- **A3 Mechanism diagnostics:** (i) attention entropy / effective-context metric vs position in long windows (context-degradation signature); (ii) FFT of learned Hyena filters overlaid on data PSD (which head captures which scale \u2014 tests the multiscale-head claim in H2); (iii) failure-mode taxonomy per arm at its G* (spectral roll-off, local-detail blur, collapse, divergence).\n- **A4 Sensitivity:** #Hyena heads / filter-length schedule vs number of data timescales (sweep on S1/S2); block-size B effect on consistency (protocol sensitivity).\n- **A5 One-shot vs blockwise (C5):** separates protocol artifact from model capability.\n- **A6 Real-data (if included):** same battery on mocap/weather; note finite-length ceiling on G.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility statement (design-only)\n- **Explicit scope:** This is a design document. NO experiment was run; no results exist or are fabricated. The task states repositories and compute are not required; execution is out of scope. Any future execution requires installing dependencies (current env: Python 3.14, no numpy/scipy/torch), which is fine because the deliverable is the design.\n- **CPU pilot path** (feasible on the 224-core CPU node, if ever run): minimal numpy-based implementation; d_model \u2248 64, N \u2264 6 blocks, \u03c4 = 2, L_train \u2248 512, synthetic S1+S2 only, 20k steps, 3 seeds; purpose: validate pipeline, verify metric thresholds are computable, measure pilot wall-clock.\n- **GPU full-scale path:** d_model 192\u2013512, N 6\u201312, L_train 1024 (synthetic) / 512 (mocap), G up to 2^14, 200k steps, 5\u20138 seeds; requires a GPU node and PyTorch/Hyena tooling.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Assumptions (explicitly flagged, NOT facts) and locked design decisions\n\nAssumptions reconstructed from general knowledge (not confirmable from workspace sources):\n1. **SiT = Stochastic Interpolant Transformers (Tong et al., NeurIPS 2024)** in a trajectory-forecasting reading (conditioned generation of trajectory segments via interpolant velocity-field regression). Alternative reading (SiT as a different time-series transformer) would change only \u00a72.1/\u00a74.1 wording, not the design skeleton.\n2. **\"Time attention\" = the transformer's temporal self-attention** over the trajectory axis; whether the timestep/flow conditioning path (adaLN-Zero) is also part of \"time attention\" is unknown \u2014 design decision: conditioning path is left untouched by the intervention, so setup_4 vs setup_5 differ ONLY in the temporal mixing module.\n3. **Causality of the baseline during generation is unspecified in sources** \u2014 locked as a design decision: within-block non-causal self-attention for setup_4 (to match setup_5's non-causality and the blockwise protocol), with causal-attention control C3 to re-test the choice.\n4. Study-context setups are **only the two lines given** in the brief; all implementation details (masking, parameter counts, datasets, exact SiT variant) are our specifications.\n\nLocked design decisions: (D1) within-block non-causal baseline attention; (D2) blockwise fill/extend generation identical for all arms; (D3) time/flow conditioning path excluded from the intervention; (D4) thresholds in \u00a75; (D5) synthetic simulators S1\u2013S5 as the primary evidence, real data optional; (D6) matched parameter/steps/seeds across arms.\n\nOperationalizations (locked): \"extremely long\" = G \u2265 8\u00d7 L_train and/or G \u2265 2^14 steps; \"consistent\" = V3+V4+V5 thresholds; \"multiple timescales\" = F1\u2013F6 within thresholds. All operationalizations carry explicit numeric bounds (Section 5) so the question is decidable.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Residual uncertainties / items needing clarification\n- Exact content of NeurIPS 2024 paper 93022 (setup_4/setup_5 tables, SiT variant, datasets, original metrics) \u2014 not derivable from workspace and not fetchable (no network).\n- Whether real datasets (motion capture / weather) are in scope and obtainable; design handles neither case.\n- The precise Hyena hyperparameter mapping (orders m, filter-length schedule) in the original study, if different from Poli et al.'s multiscale convention \u2014 our sweep A4 absorbs this.\n- Whether the study intends pointwise forecast accuracy or distributional/spectral fidelity as the success criterion \u2014 we measure both and gate the claim on the pre-registered rule.", "evidence": "Reproducible inspection records: /workspace contains only task.json (question text + \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"); no paper source/table/code for the referenced study (setup_4/setup_5) exists on disk; environment checks show Python 3.14.4, 224 CPUs, no GPU, no network (urllib to example.com times out/fails), and no numpy/scipy/torch or hyena/einops/hydra packages installed; no datasets mounted; filesystem scan found no relevant source documents. This confirms the deliverable must be a design document built from the brief's two setup lines plus public-domain knowledge of SiT (Stochastic Interpolant Transformers, Tong et al.) and the Hyena operator (Poli et al., ICML 2023: input gating + implicit FFT long convolution, O(L log L), state-space/S4-family relationship, causal vs non-causal filter truncation, multiscale heads), with all non-derivable specifics explicitly flagged as assumptions or locked design decisions.", "assumptions": ["SiT = Stochastic Interpolant Transformers (Tong et al., NeurIPS 2024) in a trajectory-forecasting reading; alternative readings change only module-level wording.", "'Time attention' = temporal self-attention; the timestep/flow conditioning (adaLN-Zero) path is excluded from the intervention (locked decision D3).", "Baseline causality during generation is unspecified by sources; locked as within-block non-causal attention (D1) with causal control C3.", "Study-context setups are only the two brief lines; all implementation details are our specifications.", "Real datasets are optional; primary evidence is synthetic simulators S1-S5 (unbounded length, known spectra/invariants).", "This is a design-only deliverable; execution out of scope; CPU pilot feasible only after installing numpy/scipy; full-scale needs GPU.", "Operationalizations locked with numeric bounds: 'extremely long' = G>=8x L_train and/or >=2^14 steps; 'consistent' = V3+V4+V5 thresholds; 'multiple timescales' = F1-F6 thresholds."], "uncertainties": ["Exact content of NeurIPS 2024 paper 93022 (setup_4/setup_5 tables, SiT variant, original datasets/metrics) \u2014 not derivable from workspace, not fetchable (no network).", "Whether real trajectory datasets (motion capture/weather) are in scope and obtainable; design covers both cases.", "Original study's exact Hyena hyperparameters (orders, filter schedules) if different from Poli et al.'s multiscale convention; absorbed by sweep A4.", "Whether success is judged by pointwise forecast accuracy vs distributional/spectral fidelity; we measure both and gate on the pre-registered rule."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-6d406553f5604d8aa4d6352bb4f566dc", "secur